-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Every deck gets one of these results:

  PASS        expected to recover, and did
  DEGRADED    expected to degrade, and did. A known, documented
              failure of the algorithm; its picture shows what it
              looks like.
  REFUSED     expected to be refused by input validation, and was,
              for the reason in its "# reason :" line
  KNOWN       a DOCUMENTED failure, as described by the deck's
              "# expected :" line. Not a fault in the test, and nothing
              to fix to keep the suite green; the deck notes explain it.
  UNEXPECTED  expected to degrade, but recovered: the failure boundary
              has moved. Investigate, then update the deck.
  FIXED       a known failure that did NOT happen. Good news, but the
              deck now misdescribes the code: remove its "# known" and
              "# expected" lines.
  FAIL        anything else: a crash, a missing nonconvex warning, a
              HARD check broken, a recovered deck over tolerance, a
              deck refused for the wrong reason or not refused

  The exit status is nonzero on any FAIL, UNEXPECTED or FIXED. It answers
  "does the suite still describe the algorithm correctly", not "did
  every mesh come out right".

  HARD checks (any h): no element centroid outside the polygon (the
  clip deletes exactly those), no inverted elements, no element naming
  a missing node.

  Recovery is judged on MISMATCH: the area of the mesh outside the
  polygon (overhang) plus the polygon area the mesh does not cover
  (uncovered), from exact polygon/triangle clipping. Recovered means
  mismatch <= 0.5 % of the polygon area. Net area is also reported but
  is not used: overhang and uncovered cancel in it.

  Also reported: polygon vertices that are not mesh nodes. A notch tip
  that is not a node cannot be reproduced at any resolution.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PICTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  orange      polygon area NOT covered by the mesh       (uncovered)
  red         mesh area reaching OUTSIDE the polygon     (overhang)
  grey        elements wholly inside the polygon
  black line  the true outline
  blue ring   a polygon vertex that is not a mesh node

